KLIN CODING CONVENTIONS
-----------------------

How errors are returned, who frees what, and which state is global. New
code follows these; old code that does not is a bug to fix when touched.


RETURN VALUES

    int status functions      0 on success, ~0 on error
    ssize_t / size functions  the count, or -1 on error
    predicates (is_*, has_*)  1 or 0; they cannot fail (a bad argument is
                              "no"). Never return 0 for both "no" and
                              "error": write a status function instead
    pointer getters           NULL when absent; say in the comment whether
                              NULL can also mean an error

A function that can fail returns a status; results go out through
pointer arguments, written only on success.


ERROR MACROS (LibU, toolbox/carpal.h)

    dbg_err_if(e)    an error that should not happen: log (debug builds)
                     and goto err
    warn_err_if(e)   an error worth a warning in production (bad
    warn_err_ifm()   configuration, a client error we report)
    crit_err_if(e)   the process cannot go on usefully
    nop_err_if(e)    an expected outcome that uses the error path (e.g.
                     "no match"): no log
    dbg_if(e)        log and carry on. Only where ignoring the failure is
                     intended; say why in a comment

Inside an err: block, use dbg_if(), never dbg_err_if(): the latter jumps
back to err.


OWNERSHIP

The creator of an object frees it with the matching *_free(). A function
that keeps a pointer it was given says so in its comment ("takes
ownership", or "the caller keeps it, and it must outlive x").

    request_t, response_t   one per request, created and freed by
                            http_serve_request(); free the request first
                            (its io flush uses the response)
    header_t                owned by its request or response
    field_t                 owned by the header it was added to;
                            header_del_field() hands it back to the caller,
                            who frees it with field_free()
    session_t               created per request by session_create(), freed
                            with session_free()
    vars_t / var_t          a request's args list owns its vars; args_get
                            and args_post hold the same var_t pointers
                            without owning them (see request_vars())
    io_t                    io_free() frees the codecs attached to it and
                            closes the descriptor only if created with
                            IO_FD_CLOSE
    codec_t                 owned by the io_t it was added to
    vhost_t                 owned by the http_t; its cached strings (alias,
                            rq_temp_dir) are copies it frees
    talarm_t                timerm_add() returns it; timerm_del() frees it.
                            A fired alarm is off the list but still
                            allocated: timerm_del() it (or U_FREE it)

Configuration (u_config_t) is read when the server starts and is not
changed afterwards: caches such as vhost_t's may keep values read from it.


GLOBAL STATE

klind is single-threaded; a worker serves one connection at a time.
Process-wide state, and what happens to it in a forked child:

    ctx (context.h)           shared; the child sets ctx->pipc
    timer list (timer.c)      a child drops the parent's alarms the first
                              time it sets one (timerm_adopt); the cached
                              pid is reset by pthread_atfork (glibc/macOS)
    field and response-filter caches (field.c, rsfilter.c)
                              per process, inherited as they are (they only
                              hold free memory)
    server watchdog and spare flags (server.c)
                              set in the child by server_spawn_child

Signal handlers only set flags (volatile sig_atomic_t) and return; the
main loop acts on them. The timer's SIGALRM handler is the exception for
now: it runs the alarm callbacks, which must therefore not allocate or log.


NAMES AND TEXT

Public functions are prefixed with their module (request_, response_,
header_, field_, http_, server_ ...). C99, 4-space indents, lines up to 80
columns. Comments say why, not what. Messages and comments that mention
the KLone name are history, and keep it.
